[#:seon.task{:name "a-slow-reply-names-nothing"
             :intent "The SCI reply's `:seon.repl/ms` is the arm's evaluation time only (repl.clj ~218-219): acquisition (339 ms on a probe, recorded as `:seon.eval/acquisition-ms` but not shown), the install gate's disposable branch, and settlement are absent, and no armed function is named. pid 66629's 82 s evaluations left nothing on the cluster (max recorded duration 471 ms). Record [sym ms] of armed calls over slow-ms into the evaluating kernel arm (already per evaluation and carried across threads, adopt-arm), settle them beside acquisition-ms, and show the whole wall time and those names when it exceeds one second; the JVM-wide window it replaced is gone. Done when a regression whose evaluation waits over one second on an armed call gets a reply naming that call and the whole wall time, and concurrent work in other arms is not named. Read `seon.repl/response-entries`, `seon.sci.kernel/adopt-arm` and `docs/research/agent-platform/perf-audit-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "every-reply-repeats-open-faults"
             :intent "Each SCI reply's :errors repeated the agent's same open fault (`seon.schema.datahike/decode-vectors`, occurrence count 1..17) on every evaluation for the whole session, about 150 tokens per reply, whether or not the evaluation caused it, and after the fault's last occurrence. Show a fault in the reply of the evaluation that produced it or once when it opens; later replies can name the open count. More evidence (default, 2026-10-08): seon.cluster.registry/retire-branch! \"Connection has been released.\" (layer :seon.error/normalization, no task) from an unrelated operation appeared twice on a (keys result/...) reply. Also see seon.test/test-release-closes-branch-connections-out-of-order. Read `seon.repl/response` and `docs/research/agent-platform/repl-dogfood-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-slow-reply-names-counts-not-proportionality"
             :intent "A reply over one second lists :repeated [[sym calls ms] ...] sorted by call count (request-merge!: `seon.schema.datahike/logical-attribute?` 199,406 calls first) but not what the work was proportional to or which armed call held the wall time, so the agent cannot tell a hub traversal from a per-member recording. Order by inclusive time, name the top call's input size (members, datoms, program entities), and state the ratio to the evaluation's own input. Read `seon.profile/summary`, `seon.repl/response-entries` and `docs/research/agent-platform/dogfood-loop-2026-09-28.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "offered-operations-cannot-name-the-prior-result"
             :intent "*1, *2 and *3 are nil at the SCI REPL (dogfood loop 2026-09-28), so a value that offers its next call as data cannot refer to itself: `seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!` offers (`seon.cluster.source/accept-merge!` {...}) with its whole request literal. Bind *1/*2/*3 (and *e) in the agent's context from its own evaluations, as clojure.main does, or have the reply's printer substitute the result symbol it already prints (result/eN) into offered forms, so an offer is short and runs as shown. Read `seon.repl/response` and `docs/research/agent-platform/dogfood-loop-2026-09-28.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "directory-render-returns-text-the-printer-clips-mid-form"
             :intent "render-directory-ai (src/seon/repl.clj) is dir's `:seon.render/ai` face but returns pr-str text (contract :string), so the value printer charges it as one leaf and clips it by characters: (dir my.message) in a fresh cluster is shown as 3,199 characters ending \"render-a…+646\" inside an unclosed schema map, which no reader can read back (EOF while reading), and its tail rows and `:seon.repl/schemas` are unreachable data. Return the {`:seon.repl/columns` `:seon.repl/rows` `:seon.repl/schemas`} map itself so the printer spreads its one budget over the shape and names each cut with a dig form; the HTML face renders the same map (test-data-assertions lane, 2026-09-28). Read `seon.render.value/render-ai`, `seon.repl/render-directory-ai`, `seon.repl/render-directory-html` and `test/seon/supplied_documentation_test.clj`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-jvm-def-is-told-it-lives-in-sci"
             :intent "A def evaluated in MCP JVM mode (a host Var in a real namespace) carries no note now that `seon.repl/def-note` reads `:seon.eval/private` (the batch's SCI decision, never recorded in JVM mode); it should be told it is the host Var and lasts until the JVM restarts, from a decision recorded by the JVM-mode batch. History: it was told it \"lives only in your SCI context\", which is false for a host Var, and the note was printed beside a def whose evaluation failed (observed 2026-09-28, flow-processes-opus: (def routing (... inst ...)) with inst unresolved returned the compile error and the persistence note). Decide the note from the evaluation's surface (SCI vs JVM) and its outcome, not from the text: no note on a failed def; a JVM def names the host Var and that it lasts until the JVM restarts. Read `seon.repl/def-note` and `seon.repl/text`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-def-bound-to-an-error-value-shows-only-the-var",
            :intent
            "(def tasks (`seon.db/q` '[:find [(pull ?t [... `:seon.task/sym` ...]) ...] ...] db)) answered {`:seon.render.var/symbol` tasks} with no error, though the query refused (uninstalled `:seon.task/sym`); the next form (count tasks) answered 13, the error map's key count, and (map `:seon.task/ns` tasks) thirteen nils. Done when a def whose value is an error map shows that error (its type and message) beside the Var, asserted through the agent entrance. Read `seon.render.var/render-ai` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/context-design/reference-kinds-notes.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "a-ref-from-another-branch-prints-with-the-wrong-identity",
            :intent
            "A JVM-mode reply that shows a value pulled from ANOTHER branch prints its entity refs as lookup refs resolved against the evaluating agent's own branch: `(d/pull @(seon.db/branch-connection @(seon.cluster.boot/connection \"default\") :agent-cfd70b5547f7) '[{:seon.agent/settings [*]}] [:seon.agent/id \"r-turn-a\"])` run as agent r-turn (branch :agent-21293ca0b763) showed `:seon.config/agent [:seon.cluster.eval/id \"732b426d6067\"]`; eid 125951 is agent r-turn-a on the pulled branch and an evaluation on r-turn's branch. Turn refs printed the same way (`:seon.turn/agent [:seon.cluster.eval/id ...]`, `:seon.turn/trigger [:seon.turn/id ...]`). Resolve eids against the database the value came from, or print the eid. Proof: a deftest pulling from a second branch and asserting the shown ref. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-turn-notes.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "every-def-repeats-the-same-persistence-note",
            :intent
            "Every SCI def reply carries the same :note sentence (about 60 tokens: 'x lives only in your SCI context and is lost when the JVM restarts...'), from `seon.repl/def-note`; one scratch session of 25 defs carried it 25 times, the largest repeated text in the context. Done when the note is shown once per agent session (or once per conversation) and later def replies carry no note, asserted on reply data. Read `seon.repl/response` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/context-design/reference-kinds-notes.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "printed-output-reaches-the-reply-unbounded"
             :intent "A form's printed output reaches the reply whole: eval_clj sci '(dotimes [i 300000] (println i))' answered a 2,289,210-character MCP reply (:out-tokens 621528) on default 2026-10-08, while a 2,000,000-character string VALUE answered 5,172 characters. seon.repl's response composes `:seon.repl/out` with `seon.print/string-literal`, a second printer outside the value printer's budget; the only response is a :large-output finding beside the flood. Correct: :out is shown through the value printer's one budget with a dig form for the rest, like any value. Proof: a deftest asserting the reply of 300,000 printed lines is under the value printer's bound and names a dig form. Read `seon.repl/response` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/repl-audit/repl-audit-opus-audit.md`."}

 #:seon.task{:name "the-out-elision-form-re-pulls-the-database"
             :intent "A cut :out prints (subs (`:seon.cluster.eval/output` (`seon.db/pull` '[`:seon.cluster.eval/output`] [`:seon.cluster.eval/id` \"29ea417f846d\"])) 4669) (default 2026-10-08): a database read with a Seon attribute name while the live output exists in the runtime. shown-output hands the printer a `seon.db/pull` requery id. Owner ruling 2026-10-08: elision forms are plain clojure.core on the live result symbol, with no database re-pull while the live value exists. Done when the form is (subs (:out result/e…) n), or (subs out/e… n) on the live output, asserted by evaluating it through the MCP entrance and comparing with (subs output n). Read `seon.repl/shown-output` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/printer-rules/printer-rules-opus-printer-rules.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reply-lacks-gc-commits-and-a-dialled-allocation-bound"
             :intent "Each SCI reply now shows :seon.repl/alloc-mb from :seon.eval/allocated-bytes (every thread's allocation over the evaluation's window, since SCI runs on a virtual thread) and a :large-allocation finding past :seon.config.eval/bounds (400 MB). Missing: the evaluation's GC count and collection-time delta and the commits its request wrote, which need new :seon.eval attributes carried by seon.sci.eval's evaluation record and seon.turn's settlement and pull list (held files when this was filed); and the bound is declared data in the bounds schema, not a :seon.config/dial an operator can size for the machine, because seon.repl/findings judges the emission alone and receives no configuration. Done when a reply that collected garbage shows its GC delta, an eval request shows its commit count, and changing a dial changes the :large-allocation threshold without a reload. Read `seon.profile/measure`, `seon.repl/findings` and `seon.sci.kernel/arm`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-request-spends-seconds-outside-its-named-stages"
             :intent "`:seon.repl/request` `:seon.cost/ms` exceeds the sum of its `:seon.cost/stages` by seconds, naming nothing: `(doc my.edit/exact!)` on agent repl-only-2 took 10,594 ms with stages summing about 290 ms; a defn plus `my.program/ns-unmap!` request 33,137 ms against about 12,000 ms of stages; `my.program/write-branch!` 8,028 ms against about 750 ms (default 35e95e7b3, 2026-10-09). Done when every request's stages account for its wall time within a stated tolerance, the remainder named as a stage. Read `seon.repl/findings` and `seon.turn/step`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false"
             :intent "`seon.repl/token-boundary?`, the private predicate `seon.repl/syntax-tokens` asks at every character, answers the character itself or nil instead of true or false: its body is `(or (Character/isWhitespace character) (some #{character} \"()[]{}\\\";,`~@^'\"))`, so `(token-boundary? \\()` answers `\\(` and `(token-boundary? \\a)` answers nil (observed in the JVM on default 6299bcda4: each of the fifteen delimiters answers itself, whitespace answers true, and \\a, \\1 and \\\\ answer nil). It is uncontracted. A token boundary is exactly a whitespace character (Character/isWhitespace) or one of the fifteen delimiters ( ) [ ] { } \" ; , ` ~ @ ^ ' and for those it answers true and for every other character false, so `seon.repl/syntax-tokens` answers exactly the tokens it answers today, which `seon.repl-test/colouring-preserves-every-character-including-malformed-source` proves. Its contract names the existing JVM predicate `clojure.core/char?` in a `[:fn …]` with an :error/message and bounded :gen/elements, output `:boolean`; the property generates characters with `clojure.test.check.generators/char` and checks them with `clojure.test.check/quick-check` under `seon.test-support/assert-check!`. Read `seon.repl/token-boundary?`, `seon.repl/syntax-tokens` and `seon.repl-test/colouring-preserves-every-character-including-malformed-source`."
             :children #{[:seon.task/sym seon.repl/prove-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false]
                         [:seon.task/sym seon.repl/implement-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false]}}
 #:seon.task{:name "prove-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false"
             :step :prove
             :product #{seon.repl-test/token-boundary-is-true-exactly-for-whitespace-and-the-fifteen-delimiters
                        seon.repl-test/every-token-boundary-answer-is-a-boolean}
             :instructions "In `seon.repl-test`, beside `seon.repl-test/colouring-preserves-every-character-including-malformed-source`, add two deftests, the step's declared product. Call the private predicate through its Var, `(#'seon.repl/token-boundary? c)`. (1) A table named token-boundary-is-true-exactly-for-whitespace-and-the-fifteen-delimiters, `[character expected]` cases asserted with `clojure.core/=` on `(#'seon.repl/token-boundary? character)`: true for \\( \\) \\[ \\] \\{ \\} \\\" \\; \\, \\` \\~ \\@ \\^ \\' \\space \\newline \\tab, false for \\a \\Z \\1 \\: \\# \\\\ \\- \\_ \\. (the delimiter rows and the false rows are red today, since they answer the character or nil; the whitespace rows are green). Its assertion message is short fixed prose, never a printed value. (2) A property named every-token-boundary-answer-is-a-boolean, written like the existing one with `clojure.test.check/quick-check` 300 and a fixed `:seed` (use 20261013) under `seon.test-support/assert-check!`: for every character c from `clojure.test.check.generators/char`, `(clojure.core/boolean? (#'seon.repl/token-boundary? c))`. Tag both `^{:seon/tasks #{'seon.repl/implement-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false}}`. Run them red on your branch: the table's delimiter and false rows and the property (seed 20261013 fails at its first test) are red; the whitespace rows and the existing colouring test are green."}
 #:seon.task{:name "implement-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false"
             :step :implement
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.repl/prove-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false]}
             :instructions "Make the table and the property green by changing `seon.repl/token-boundary?` only: it answers true for a whitespace character or one of the fifteen delimiters and false otherwise (for example `clojure.core/boolean` around the existing test, or `clojure.core/contains?` on a set of the delimiters), so `seon.repl/syntax-tokens` answers exactly what it answers today and `seon.repl-test/colouring-preserves-every-character-including-malformed-source` stays green. Give it the complete contract `{:malli/schema [:=> [:cat [:fn {:error/message \"should be a character\" :gen/elements [\\a \\( \\space \\;]} clojure.core/char?]] :boolean]}` and a one-line docstring saying what it answers."}]
